    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_db76b1498bfbb875cdb75565263a714bf2f91a4a2e4d975f5374604858dec669",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The conventional interval method repeatedly generates a large prime set near each target interval and covers targets by its small-prime shifts. Reversing the translated set to P+q lets one precompute the smaller-prime set P once, vary only consecutive primes q near the interval base, and realize each union as a shifted bit-array OR; the same coverage condition remains exhaustive while the expensive object becomes reusable.",
